 "public_summary": "This is a short 1.05-day flat-bottomed dip seen 673 times on a small 3335 K M dwarf, squarely on the target with no even/odd difference or secondary eclipse. The star itself spins at the same period with strong spot modulation, which explains the large outside-dip swing and the large measured depth changes, but does not make the flat-bottom shape. The best explanation remains a ~2.7 Earth-radius planet; the depth scatter is likely spot/activity systematics and needs simultaneous ground-based photometry and spectroscopy to confirm."
